# DESCRIPTION

A process's address space is a tree of regions (VMARs).
Every process has a root VMAR spanning the user part of the address space; its handle has TRANSFER, INSPECT, READ, WRITE and EXECUTE.
A region holds mappings of VMOs and sub-regions.
All addresses and lengths here are multiples of the page size.
Mappings are placed first fit with an unmapped guard page on each side, or at an address the caller names.
No mapping is writable and executable at once except in a JIT reservation.

## vmar_allocate

`vmar_allocate` creates a sub-region of the VMAR <parent> and writes its handle to <out_child> and its base address to <out_addr>.
The call's first argument packs the handle and the options: <parent> in the low 32 bits and the options in the high 32.
The options are:

: `CROI_VM_CAN_MAP_READ`, `CROI_VM_CAN_MAP_WRITE`, `CROI_VM_CAN_MAP_EXECUTE`
  What may be mapped in the region.
  The parent's handle must have the matching rights (`CROI_ERR_ACCESS_DENIED`).
  The child's handle has TRANSFER, INSPECT and DESTROY, and READ, WRITE and EXECUTE as asked.
: `CROI_VM_SPECIFIC`
  Place it at <offset> bytes from the parent's base.
  Without it <offset> must be 0 and the region is placed first fit.
: `CROI_VM_RESERVATION`
  A region that holds views only (vmar_map_view), no mappings and no sub-regions.
  An unviewed page faults.
: `CROI_VM_JIT`
  A reservation for generated code.
  On amd64 with protection keys it gets a key of its own and its views may be readable, writable and executable together, with writes opened per thread by WRPKRU.
  Elsewhere, or when the keys are used up, it is views only and never read-write-execute.
  object_get_info(2) `CROI_INFO_VMAR_JIT` tells which.

<size> must be a nonzero multiple of the page size.
A range already in use is `CROI_ERR_ALREADY_EXISTS`; no room is `CROI_ERR_NO_MEMORY`; a parent that is a reservation, or any option not listed, is `CROI_ERR_INVALID_ARGS`.

## vmar_map

`vmar_map` maps up to 64 items into <vmar>, in order, each a `croi_vmar_map_item_t` of 48 bytes:

```c
typedef struct {
  uint32_t vmo;
  uint32_t options;      // CROI_VM_PERM_* and placement
  uint64_t vmar_offset;
  uint64_t vmo_offset;
  uint64_t len;
  uint64_t addr;         // out: where it was mapped
  int32_t status;        // out: 0, or why this item failed
  uint32_t reserved;     // 0
} croi_vmar_map_item_t;
```

It stops at the first item that fails, because a program's segments are all or nothing.
It returns how many it mapped and writes each processed item's <status> and, on success, <addr>.
<options> is `CROI_VM_PERM_READ`, `CROI_VM_PERM_WRITE` and `CROI_VM_PERM_EXECUTE` in some combination (at least one), with:

: `CROI_VM_SPECIFIC`
  Map at <vmar_offset> from the VMAR's base, in a range wholly inside it and free.
  Without it <vmar_offset> must be 0.
: `CROI_VM_REQUIRE_NON_RESIZABLE`
  `CROI_ERR_NOT_SUPPORTED` if the VMO is resizable.
: `CROI_VM_ALLOW_FAULTS`
  Accepted and ignored.
: `CROI_VM_PERM_GUARDED`
  On arm64 with branch target identification, an executable mapping is a guarded page.
  Accepted and ignored elsewhere.

The VMAR's handle needs READ, WRITE and EXECUTE for the permissions asked, and the VMO's handle needs MAP and the same.
The mapping's permissions can later be raised by vmar_protect only up to the rights of the VMO's handle.
Writable with executable is `CROI_ERR_INVALID_ARGS`, as is a misaligned or empty range and a range the VMO does not hold.
A VMAR that is a reservation refuses the call.
`CROI_ERR_ALREADY_EXISTS` is a range in use, `CROI_ERR_NO_MEMORY` no room.

`croi_vmar_map` maps one and returns 0, the item's status or the call's; it writes the address to <addr> unless it is null.

## vmar_unmap, vmar_protect

Both take up to 64 ranges, each a `croi_vmar_range_t` of 24 bytes:

```c
typedef struct {
  uint64_t addr;
  uint64_t len;
  uint32_t options;      // protect: CROI_VM_PERM_*; unmap: 0
  int32_t status;        // out: 0, or why this range failed
} croi_vmar_range_t;
```

Each range is done on its own: a range that fails does not stop the next.
The calls return how many succeeded and write each range's <status>.

`vmar_unmap` removes the mappings in the range; a mapping that crosses an edge is trimmed and one that spans the range is split.
The range must lie inside the VMAR.
<options> must be 0.
Regions stay.

`vmar_protect` changes the permissions of everything mapped in the range, which must be mapped throughout (`CROI_ERR_NOT_FOUND`), splitting mappings at the edges.
The VMAR's handle needs the rights for the new permissions.
Permissions beyond what the mapping was allowed are `CROI_ERR_ACCESS_DENIED`; writable with executable is `CROI_ERR_INVALID_ARGS`.

`croi_vmar_unmap` and `croi_vmar_protect` do one range and return 0, the range's status or the call's.

## vmar_destroy

`vmar_destroy` removes a region, its sub-regions and everything mapped in them.
The handle needs DESTROY.
The root VMAR cannot be destroyed (`CROI_ERR_ACCESS_DENIED`).

## vmar_unmap_handle_close_thread_exit

`vmar_unmap_handle_close_thread_exit` is how a thread frees its own stack and ends.
It unmaps [<addr>, <addr> + <len>) of <vmar> as vmar_unmap does, closes the handle <close_handle>, and exits the calling thread.
It does not return on success.
If unmapping fails it returns that status with the handle still open.
If <close_handle> cannot be closed, the thread takes an undefined-instruction exception (exception(2)); if a handler resumes it, the call returns `CROI_ERR_BAD_HANDLE`.
The unmap may take the caller's own stack, so after it the thread runs no user code again.

## vmar_op_range

`vmar_op_range` does <op> on the mappings in [<addr>, <addr> + <len>), <len> rounded up to a page and not 0.
<buffer> and <buffer_size> must be 0.

| op | Right on the VMAR | Does |
| `CROI_VMAR_OP_COMMIT` | WRITE | commits the pages |
| `CROI_VMAR_OP_DECOMMIT` | WRITE | frees the pages of anonymous VMOs |
| `CROI_VMAR_OP_ZERO` | WRITE | zeroes the range |
| `CROI_VMAR_OP_MAP_RANGE` | READ | enters the pages that are present in the page tables, so they do not fault |
| `CROI_VMAR_OP_PREFETCH` | READ | asks the pager for the pages |
| `CROI_VMAR_OP_DONT_NEED` | none | the hint of vmo(2) |
| `CROI_VMAR_OP_ALWAYS_NEED` | none | the hint of vmo(2) |

The range must be mapped throughout (`CROI_ERR_BAD_STATE` if there is a gap) and lie inside the VMAR (`CROI_ERR_OUT_OF_RANGE`).
The mappings must have the permissions the op needs (`CROI_ERR_ACCESS_DENIED`).
Commit, decommit and prefetch work only on the VMAR's own mappings, not those of sub-regions; the other ops reach sub-regions if the handle has OP_CHILDREN.
Otherwise a mapping in a sub-region is `CROI_ERR_INVALID_ARGS`.
The first error stops the call, with the earlier pieces done.
Any other op is `CROI_ERR_INVALID_ARGS`.

## vmar_map_view

`vmar_map_view` shows [<vmo_offset>, <vmo_offset> + <len>) of a VMO at <vmar_offset> in a reservation, replacing whatever views were there, atomically: other threads see the old view or the new one, never a hole.
The first argument packs the handle and the permissions: <vmar> in the low 32 bits and `CROI_VM_PERM_*` in the high 32.
The VMAR must be a reservation, and the range inside it (`CROI_ERR_INVALID_ARGS`).
Rights are as for vmar_map; the VMO's handle also gives the ceiling for a later vmar_protect.
Writable with executable is allowed only in a JIT reservation that has a protection key.
An executable view needs a VMO handle with EXECUTE; see vmo(2).
vmar_unmap and vmar_protect work on the views of a reservation, and the range stays reserved.

